1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,875
My name is James Rosing.

2
00:00:01,875 --> 00:00:09,375
I'm a surgeon in Newport Beach,
California, and for more than fourteen
years I've been getting letters like
this one.

3
00:00:09,375 --> 00:00:17,250
The letter is fake; the reason is real:
"not medically necessary." A payer's
software writes a denial in about a
second.

4
00:00:17,250 --> 00:00:19,500
Appealing it takes a physician hours.

5
00:00:19,500 --> 00:00:24,000
So most denials never get appealed, and
the patient ends up paying.

6
00:00:24,000 --> 00:00:28,235
Some say AI cancels out here, because
both sides can buy the same model.

7
00:00:28,235 --> 00:00:30,050
But nobody can buy the chart.

8
00:00:30,050 --> 00:00:34,588
A denial is a claim about the payer's
policy, and the payer holds the policy.

9
00:00:34,588 --> 00:00:39,429
An appeal is a claim about the medical
record, and only the physician holds the
record.

10
00:00:39,429 --> 00:00:43,361
The payer's model never sees the chart,
so it can't write the appeal.

11
00:00:43,361 --> 00:00:44,874
The imbalance isn't in intelligence.

12
00:00:44,874 --> 00:00:45,782
It's in evidence.

13
00:00:45,782 --> 00:00:52,437
This project gives the advantage to the
side with the evidence: if the chart
passes a deterministic gate, an appeal
gets drafted.

14
00:00:52,437 --> 00:00:55,160
Here's one of fifty synthetic cases, no
real patients.

15
00:00:55,160 --> 00:01:00,000
A 47-year-old woman needs radiation
planning after a lumpectomy, and her
Medicare Advantage plan denied it.

16
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:10,161
Muse Spark 1.3 first asks my coverage
index when the plan's rules last
changed, then opens only the entries it
needs.

17
00:01:10,161 --> 00:01:15,000
Every sentence it writes quotes one
entry and names it.

18
00:01:15,000 --> 00:01:25,658
As each sentence comes in, a
deterministic gate checks that the quote
really appears in that entry, and that
every number, date, and code matches the
source.

19
00:01:25,658 --> 00:01:30,000
Here, every sentence was verified, in
twelve seconds, for two cents.

20
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:34,468
Now I'll change one number in a verified
sentence, and the gate refuses it.

21
00:01:34,468 --> 00:01:37,340
The model writes the draft, but the
record decides.

22
00:01:37,340 --> 00:01:45,000
The payer can run the same gate on my
appeal, so the dispute gets settled on
the record, not on whose bot writes
better.

23
00:01:45,000 --> 00:01:49,889
When the chart can't support an
argument, the model says so.

24
00:01:49,889 --> 00:01:57,000
Here, Muse Wonder read all twenty-two
entries looking for a sepsis criterion,
found nothing, and declined.

25
00:01:57,000 --> 00:02:05,211
Across fifty cases, three Meta models
including the open-weight one, and nine
runs, agreement with the answer key
ranged from forty-four to fifty out of
fifty.

26
00:02:05,211 --> 00:02:07,421
Not one sentence contained a fabricated
quote.

27
00:02:07,421 --> 00:02:09,632
Spark's runs cost under a dollar each.

28
00:02:09,632 --> 00:02:15,000
We injected more than twelve thousand
fabrications to attack the gate, and it
caught every hard one.

29
00:02:15,000 --> 00:02:25,500
This is what the physician sees: the
appeal, the payer's reason answered
directly, every sentence footnoted to
the entry it quotes.

30
00:02:25,500 --> 00:02:30,000
It stays a draft until a human signs it.

31
00:02:30,000 --> 00:02:41,111
The gate is on npm as litmus-gate, and
either side of the table can use it.

32
00:02:41,111 --> 00:02:53,611
Next, I'll run it on my own denials, and
then on prior authorizations, the same
problem in reverse.

33
00:02:53,611 --> 00:02:55,000
Thank you.
